WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.610
My name is Mike and I'm
here to talk to you today

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.650
about interactive
graphics in R.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:13.860
A little bit about me.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.840
I code in R for a living
and I believe R can be fun.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.200
What do I mean by fun?

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.670
I mean things like

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.500
including kittens
in ggplots,

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.880
runnibng destructive
physics simulations,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.420
and currently I'm experimenting
with voxel rendering.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.040
There is fun to be had in R.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.660
And today I'd like to share
with you such a discovery.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.380
And that is with
careful use of base R,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.070
interactive
graphics are possible

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.130
and this opens the way
for new visualizations.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.363
And when I say new
visualizations,

00:00:48.363 --> 00:00:49.780
I'm going to use
it to play games.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.010
My interactive graphics
started with the game Wordle.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.860
This has an intuitive
interface with animations

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.780
and a great user experience.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.410
And so I created a
clone of this in R to play.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.650
This was a text-based interface
which feels a bit clunky.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:15.750
It does not have the feel or the
fluidity of the official game.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.210
And I realized,

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.510
R gives easy access to
text interfaces, like Zork

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.710
on the left.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.830
But I want to play actual video
games with sound and color

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.600
and graphics and
actual interaction,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.300
like Pacman on the right.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.580
So I set myself a moonshot goal.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.950
I knew where I wanted
to go, but I didn't know

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.750
how I was going to get there.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:41.200
And my moonshot was this.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.750
I will commit myself
to achieving the goal,

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.930
before this year is out,
of writing and playing

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.160
a video game in R.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.240
But this then
raises the question,

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.505
what extra things do I need to know
in R in order to get a game running?

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.820
And I needed to be
sure of three things.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.040
I'm going to discuss
this with you today,

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.990
and that is a fast
graphics device

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.770
for rendering to, a fast way of
drawing to this graphics device

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:12.095
to render the game, and mouse
and keyboard interaction

00:02:12.095 --> 00:02:13.470
so we can actually
play the game.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.780
A fast graphics device is
essential if I actually

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.500
want to play games.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.040
I need the graphics to respond
at least 30 frames per second.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.770
If not, then the games
will never run smoothly.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.430
When I say graphics device, I'm
referring to the area or window

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.100
where the plots are displayed
in R, like the plot

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.600
window in RStudio.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.240
On the Mac,

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.850
there are a few other
graphics devices

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:45.060
that are the quartz
and the X11 device

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.730
and I needed to compare them
to find out what their speed

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.020
and response times were.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.700
I wrote a very simple
benchmark to test

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.250
how fast a graphics
device would respond

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:56.665
to a simple plotting command.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.140
And I was pleasantly surprised
that the graphics devices can

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.590
respond at 30 times per second.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.620
In this plot, we see the
RStudio and the quartz device

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.890
both reaching 30 frames per
second, with the quartz device

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:15.790
about twice as fast
as the RStudio device.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.620
What was really
surprising was to see

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.980
that the X11 device can respond
to commands at over 500 times

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.090
per second.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.990
So yes, R does have a
fast graphics device. We're

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:27.290
over the first hurdle.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.980
All graphics devices
can respond fast enough

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.830
and some graphics devices
are faster than others.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.420
On the Mac, I'm going
to choose to use

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.602
the X11 device because
it is ridiculously fast.

00:03:38.602 --> 00:03:41.060
Now that I'm confident that
the graphics device can respond

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.820
quickly, I need a fast way
of drawing to that graphics

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.870
device.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.030
My focus is on
pixel-based games,

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:50.640
so blocky pixels and
polygons and character

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.955
sprites moving around,
like the game of Pac-Man.

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.330
A character sprite like
this cartoon dinosaur

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:00.430
is just a collection
of colored squares

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.170
and this can be
drawn very quickly

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.860
using just a base R graphics
command, grid rect.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.590
If I draw this, clear the
screen, and draw it again

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:11.060
over and over, I can
get some animation.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.940
To ensure this drawing
system can scale,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.339
I'm going to use a common
animation technique known

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.140
as double buffering.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.080
In double buffering,

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.170
there's one buffer for
drawing and one buffer

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.260
for rendering to the screen.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.740
And I'm going to
implement it in R like this.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.680
I'm going to create a buffer
in R, and then for every frame,

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.610
draw the game into the buffer.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.030
And when that buffer is
ready, copy that buffer

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:42.232
into the graphics device.

00:04:42.232 --> 00:04:43.190
For the drawing buffer.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:47.280
I first considered
a standard R matrix.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.470
This seems like a
pretty good match

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:54.430
for representing rectangular
array of pixels on the screen.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.920
But I ran into some challenges
that actually slowed things

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.320
down, and that is
the data ordering

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.290
and the color representation
within a matrix.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.010
In a matrix,

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.880
the data ordering is such
that sequential values define

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.650
a column. In a graphics device,

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.690
it wants sequential values
to define a row of pixels.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:21.030
So there is a mismatch between
the data representation.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:24.340
There is also a mismatch in the
color representation. In an R

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.280
matrix,

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.570
you would represent
color as a string,

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.810
but a graphics device actually
wants four numeric values

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.760
for the 4 bytes of red, green,
blue, and alpha color channels.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.560
So the matrix data
layout does not

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.600
match the graphics
device, which means

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.443
if I was to render a matrix
to the graphics device,

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:45.860
then every single
time it actually

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.840
has to do an internal
data conversion

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.380
and this is going
to slow things down.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.140
There is actually a second
rectangular data structure

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:58.630
in R that is more compatible
with graphics devices.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:05:59.880
And that is the native raster.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.350
The native raster is a built
in data structure in R,

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.220
but it is rarely used.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:09.400
It's difficult to
manipulate from R,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:14.690
but it is highly compatible
with graphics devices.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.670
This table illustrates
that the native raster

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:18.470
object and the
graphics device have

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.920
the same data ordering and
the same color representation.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.690
This means that the
native raster objects

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:26.240
will be very fast to copy
to the graphics device

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.075
as no conversion
will need to be done.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.310
So my specific double
buffered rendering technique

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:35.750
looks like this.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:38.690
I will create a native
raster buffer in R

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:41.840
then for every frame, draw
the game into that buffer

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.420
and then when it's ready, copy
that buffer to the graphics

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:48.180
device just with the
grid raster command.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.150
This is a really powerful
and scalable technique.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.020
It allows to R render multiple
sprites very quickly.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:59.320
As a demonstration that this
double buffering technique

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.120
works,

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.080
this is a live capture of
three animated dinosaurs.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.390
This is great, but three
objects aren't going

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:09.100
to make an interesting game.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.350
And as I said, this
method scales and it

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:14.360
scales to 100 dinosaurs.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.990
1,000 dinosaurs.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:22.750
And the limit

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:24.510
I could reach before
the drawing speed

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:28.370
dropped below the magic
30 frames per second

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.680
was 5,000 dinosaurs.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.400
And I was really
happy with this.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:35.960
R has a fast way of drawing.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:39.080
If I use the double buffering
technique using a native raster

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.135
as the drawing buffer
and then copy that buffer

00:07:41.135 --> 00:07:42.760
to the graphics device
when it's ready.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.650
The final piece of the
puzzle is interactivity,

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:52.620
an essential element of any game
that you actually want to play.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.630
A standard way you
currently might get feedback

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:58.290
from the user is to request
text using the read line command

00:07:58.290 --> 00:07:59.830
as shown here.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:01.460
When calling readline,

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:03.320
R cannot do anything else.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.200
It must have the
user input before it

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.345
proceeds to the next line.

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:10.430
In a similar manner,

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.020
you can get the mouse
position using grid locator.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:16.570
But again, while waiting
for the user input,

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:20.810
R cannot do anything else.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:22.430
So using this style
of interaction

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:24.620
would not work for video games.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.330
Imagine a game of
Pacman where the game

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:29.270
stopped every time you needed
to control the main character.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:31.645
It's not going to be an
interesting game or very fun.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:36.450
We have to change our
programming approach

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.750
and make use of the event-driven
capabilities of our graphics

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.860
devices. In event
driven interaction,

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:46.200
we ask the system to monitor
for events, and when they occur,

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:49.200
we ask them to run a
particular function.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.090
For those of you
familiar with Shiny,

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:53.940
it is based on an
event-driven framework.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:55.707
If you had a button
on your web page,

00:08:55.707 --> 00:08:58.290
you would not write a for loop
to check whether the button has

00:08:58.290 --> 00:08:59.900
been clicked.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.510
You would ask the system,
in this case Shiny,

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:05.370
that when the button is
clicked, please run my function.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.260
And that's what the observe
event handler is in Shiny.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:13.260
Like Shiny, R graphics
devices support

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:14.950
event-driven interaction.

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:16.270
So we don't need to monitor,

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:18.870
has the mouse moved or has
the keyboard been pressed?

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.520
We can ask the graphics
device to monitor for us.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:25.470
And when that event occurs,
run a particular function.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:28.620
This is all handled by the
grDevices package in the set

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.920
graphics event handlers function
where you can tie functions

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.680
to particular events.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:37.530
The events that
you can watch for.

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:39.240
Has the mouse moved?
And you'll get back

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.535
the coordinates of the mouse.

00:09:40.535 --> 00:09:42.660
You can monitor for the
mouse button being pressed,

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:45.302
in which case, you will
get back the button number.

00:09:45.302 --> 00:09:47.760
You can ask for the keyboard
to be monitored, in which case

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.462
you'll get which key
has been pressed.

00:09:50.462 --> 00:09:52.170
There is one extra
event to be monitored,

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:57.390
and that is the idle event, when
no other events are happening.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:59.430
Consider the idle event
to be like the heartbeat

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:02.680
of an application. When the
user is doing something,

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:05.160
yes, we handle that input
and change our values,

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.260
but if the user is
not doing anything,

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:10.980
then we run our idle
function, our heartbeat.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.680
This means that an application
skeleton might look something

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:16.450
like this.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:17.870
When the mouse moves,

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:20.470
lets update the position of
the character on the screen.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:22.420
When the keyboard
is pressed, lets

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:25.480
run a function that will check
whether the user wants to quit.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.800
And when the user
is otherwise idle,

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.900
render the next frame
in the animation.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:36.250
As proof that this event
driven interactivity works,

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:40.008
I wrote a drum machine
in R. A drum machine

00:10:40.008 --> 00:10:41.550
is just an electronic
instrument that

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:45.020
plays drums. In this
drum machine interface,

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:47.040
each row represents
an instrument,

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.380
and each button means
to play that instrument

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:52.300
at a certain moment in time.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:54.280
The events that I've
tied to this application

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:56.860
are that when the mouse
button is pressed,

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:59.830
we toggle the button
underneath the mouse

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:01.780
and when the user
is idle, we just

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:04.630
continue to play the next
beat in the sequence.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:07.900
This is a short 20 second clip
showing the first song I ever

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:08.775
composed in our.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.340
This was a lot of fun
to program and great fun

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:37.420
to experiment with and
compose my own songs.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:40.030
And it shows that event
driven interaction

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:42.370
does work for what
I want for games.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:45.760
I can simultaneously render
graphics and play sounds

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:47.325
while reacting to user input.

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:53.530
I now have all the key elements
that I need to play a game.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:56.782
The only thing left is to
actually write the game itself.

00:11:56.782 --> 00:11:58.740
And I'm not going to
write a game from scratch.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.323
I'm actually going
to port an existing

00:12:00.323 --> 00:12:02.415
game called Another World.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.580
Another World is an
action adventure side

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:11.540
scrolling platformer released
in 1991 for many platforms.

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:14.840
It has a novel for its time
virtual machine architecture,

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:16.825
which makes it easy to
port to new systems.

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:22.960
It is, in effect,
just a polygon engine.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:25.080
Rather than storing
bitmaps for scenes,

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.340
it just records a
description of how to render

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:29.450
that scene using polygons.

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:31.190
In this 20 second clip,

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:33.415
it is rendering the
first scene in the game.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.380
And the next scene is
rendering a later scene

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.785
in the game where the main
character is having a drink.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:55.620
So that's how an
individual frame

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:58.580
is rendered. For the actual
gameplay of Another World,

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:01.620
I created an R implementation
of the virtual machine.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:04.290
This was based upon the
JavaScript and the C versions,

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.997
which were originally based upon
the assembly versions for MSDOS

00:13:06.997 --> 00:13:07.580
and the Amiga.

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:12.840
The event driven game
loop is quite simple.

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:15.440
And I asked the graphics device
when the mouse or keyboard

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:16.290
are used,

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.320
it calls a function to
update the variables

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:21.650
in my virtual machine, and when
the user is otherwise idle,

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:23.820
it just executes the
next instruction.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:25.660
Maybe to draw a polygon
or play a sound.

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:29.640
Which means

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:32.210
I can show you the
demo of the game.

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:36.648
This is a capture running live
in R to preview what's coming,

00:13:36.648 --> 00:13:37.690
what you're going to see.

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:39.750
It is the typical
life of a scientist.

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:41.660
You arrive at work
in your Ferrari,

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:44.180
you drink at your desk,
and then your experiment

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:45.360
gets hit by lightning.

00:14:20.163 --> 00:14:21.580
With that strike
of lightning, you

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:23.620
are transported
to another world.

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:25.310
Later in the game,
you are captured.

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:27.650
And you're stuck inside a jail.

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:31.430
This 10 second demo illustrates
keyboard interaction.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:33.550
By using the left
and right arrow keys,

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:35.770
I am going to rock
the jail and escape.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:47.870
What's up?

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:51.430
I don't know.

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.350
That jail escape
ends quite abruptly

00:14:53.350 --> 00:14:55.190
because I have not
yet completed my goal.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:56.920
There are still bugs
to fix and a package

00:14:56.920 --> 00:14:58.990
to release before
I can claim that I

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:00.465
have a playable game in R.

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:05.605
What's next?

00:15:05.605 --> 00:15:07.230
Hopefully today with
this presentation,

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:10.380
I've managed to demonstrate that
yes, with careful use of base R,

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.510
our interactive
graphics are possible,

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:17.220
and this opens the way to play
games and new visualizations.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:20.080
I have released packages
to document this work.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:22.650
And I encourage you to try them.

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:25.480
If you want to manipulate
native raster images from R,

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:26.885
I've released the nara package.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:31.660
I've released a
friendlier wrapper

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:34.540
around the event callbacks
and the graphics devices.

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:38.850
This hopefully makes it a little
bit easier to use for games.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:40.830
I know that Matt Dray
has used it already

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:43.170
for his retro dungeon
crawler, where

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:45.510
we can see his blue
character being

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:47.315
chased by the yellow monster.

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:53.570
If you want to see an example
of a complex event driven

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:58.900
application, I have released
the drum machine as a package.

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:00.430
And I hope soon to release

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:01.460
the Another World package

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:02.960
so that everyone
can play this game.

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:09.950
R has the capability to write
and run interactive graphics

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:11.720
and games, and I'm
fascinated to see

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:14.530
what the R community can create.

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:16.000
Game on.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.790
Thank you.

